WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.415
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.415 --> 00:00:03.730
- [Narrator] Cars today
are far more complex

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.300
than ever before.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.000
They need anywhere from
several hundred semiconductors

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.770
to more than a thousand.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.440
And these chips control
everything from the ignition

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.000
to the braking system
and making sure your seat

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.236
is in the perfect position as you drive.

00:00:16.236 --> 00:00:17.980
- [Mike Colias] Individual chips,

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.440
they're packaged inside these
electronic control units.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.120
They, sort of, act as
almost, like, separate organs

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.430
that have a different
function in the human body.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:28.818
They basically tell the hardware
what to do electronically.

00:00:28.818 --> 00:00:31.250
- [Narrator] But now
auto makers can't source

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:32.870
the semiconductors fast enough

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:36.190
and many cars are sitting in
parking lots waiting for chips

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.470
because, during the
pandemic, chips for vehicles

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.040
were diverted to meet a surge
in demand for electronics

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.500
as people were stuck at home.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.790
And their availability
has become tighter also

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.950
because of natural disasters
that disrupted the supply chain

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.600
and the resurgence of COVID cases

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.890
in important chip-making regions.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:53.850
This crunch could cost global auto makers

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.970
around $110 billion in revenue this year.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.790
- This crisis has really
forced auto makers

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.810
to, kind of, rethink
both in the short term

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.230
and the longterm, how
they manage their chips

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.330
and their supply chain.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:08.610
- [Narrator] So, here's how companies

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.420
are adjusting their production plants

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.290
and what that means the next time you want

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:13.933
to drive a car off the lot.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:16.830
One of the first solutions

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.120
when you don't have enough of
something, try to use less.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.950
So some automakers are dropping
features that require chips.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.170
- Well, they're, sort of,
small add-on features,

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.494
I think people have been
willing to overlook that.

00:01:29.494 --> 00:01:31.410
- [Narrator] Some car dealers in the US

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.740
said the global auto company STELLANTIS,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.040
which owns Jeep, Ram and other brands,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.160
shipped some pickup trucks

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.000
without an Electronic Detection System,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.180
which looks out for blind spots.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.290
In another example,

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.920
GM said it was building
some full-size pickup trucks

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.130
without software that helps
manage fuel consumption.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.420
And Elon Musk said that Tesla was removing

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.290
the adjustable lumbar support
from the front passenger seat

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.590
of some vehicles due
to major industry wide

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.910
supply chain pressure.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:00.360
- It hasn't been a big hit,
sort of, reputationally

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.310
or from a market share standpoint

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.008
that they've been, kind of,
forced to take that step.

00:02:05.008 --> 00:02:06.250
- [Narrator] For instance,

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.780
STELLANTIS reported more than
$89 billion in net revenue

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.500
in the first half of this year,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.920
while General Motors had
a strong second quarter

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.363
with $2.8 billion in net profit.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.750
But auto makers have had
to make some tough choices,

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:20.987
like choosing between vehicles.

00:02:20.987 --> 00:02:24.020
- [Mike Colias] Car companies
are all trying to figure out

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.360
what, prioritize what they wanna make

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.945
and what can be sacrificed.

00:02:27.945 --> 00:02:29.350
- [Narrator] For instance,

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.930
General Motors said it's
been shifting computer chips

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.370
away from its less profitable vehicles

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.533
and using them in it's more popular ones.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.660
But even with diverting inventory,

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.010
there's no guarantee that
there will be enough chips

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.656
for the vehicles that
have been prioritized.

00:02:43.656 --> 00:02:47.210
- [Mike Colias] When they
may not have one of the chips

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:51.100
that they need, they'll continue
to build those vehicles,

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.870
and then they're setting
them aside in parking lots

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.859
around the factory and
waiting for chips to arrive.

00:02:57.859 --> 00:03:00.350
- [Narrator] This is what
some in the industry called

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.090
the build-shy strategy.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:03.850
- The good part about that strategy is,

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.320
that it allows them to
keep the factory running,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.680
'cause it's costly to keep turning off

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:09.897
and turning back on your factory.

00:03:09.897 --> 00:03:12.680
- [Narrator] But this can mean
chipless cars end up sitting

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.250
on the lot for an
indefinite amount of time.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.940
Ford said that at the end of March,

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.260
it had more than 20,000 vehicles parked

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.093
and waiting for chips.

00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:21.960
- [Mike Colias] GM over the summer,

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.420
they said they had 30,000 pickup trucks

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.033
at a plant in Missouri.

00:03:26.926 --> 00:03:29.610
- [Narrator] While these
strategies have helped companies

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.450
to keep going in the short term,

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.610
it's also forced them
to plan for the future

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:35.800
by rethinking the entire process.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.240
- For decades, the auto
industry has really, kind of,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.280
perfected this just-in-time model,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.990
where components arrive at the factory

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:48.530
and even right at the assembly
line, just as they're needed.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:52.070
That allows them to lower
their inventory costs

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.008
and there's a lot of
efficiencies that go with that.

00:03:54.008 --> 00:03:55.470
- [Narrator] But the chip shortage

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.350
has shown this model breaks
down during a global crisis

00:03:58.350 --> 00:03:59.410
like the pandemic.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:02.630
- Companies are looking
at moving to stockpiling

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.770
really crucial computer chips.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:08.860
There's been direct outreach
from, you know, auto executives

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.390
to chip suppliers, which, you know,

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:12.601
hadn't been happening in the past.

00:04:12.601 --> 00:04:14.130
- [Narrator] And auto companies

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.890
not only want more visibility
in the supply chain,

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.610
but also more direct control.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.160
- Some of the companies have talked

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.030
about even getting more involved

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.070
in designing their own chips,

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:27.010
designing components to need fewer chips.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.700
I know Ford, specifically,
has talked about that.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:30.673
- This is infrastructure.

00:04:30.673 --> 00:04:33.250
- [Narrator] There's also a
big push from the US Government

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.170
to shore up its own domestic
chip making capacity.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.840
The Biden Administration
has said it would prioritize

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.860
increasing domestic chip manufacturing

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.080
by investing roughly $50 billion

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.823
toward research and development.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.680
But building new foundries
and increasing chip production

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:49.800
will take years.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.010
And that means auto makers
will likely continue

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:53.670
to scale back production.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:54.660
In early September,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:57.730
GM said it's temporarily
idling two main factories

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.350
that produce its pickup trucks,

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:02.130
while Ford said it's
temporarily halting a production

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:05.610
of its F-150 at its Kansas
City factory in Missouri.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.470
Even some of the more chip-ready
auto makers like Toyota,

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:12.360
said it would cut production
in Japan by 40% in September.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.570
And in the short run,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.950
all this won't be good news for customers.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.850
- There just simply aren't enough cars.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.920
Usually, Americans wanna
drive off in a car that day

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:24.080
and that's not happening
nearly to the same degree

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.151
as it normally has.

00:05:25.151 --> 00:05:27.734
(upbeat music)

